review_comments:
Also, a test that helps validate the lock isn't held while invoking the callback would be good, e.g. by having the callback do something like:
```C#
var mres = new ManualResetEventSlim();
ThreadPool.QueueUserWorkItem(delegate
{
    c.Writer.TryWrite(...);
    mres.Set();
});
mres.Wait();
```
If the lock was held while invoking the callback, code like the above should deadlock.

I'm not in love with the idea of adding the extra try/finally, as it adds complexity and overhead.  A couple of alternatives:
1. Change the lock to be a try/finally, and just do the invocation as part of that try/finally after the call to Monitor.Exit.
2. Change the lock to be a try/finally, but also Exit the monitor prior to each return statement, then invoking the callback between the exit and the return, e.g.
```C#
bool releaseLock = false;
try
{
    Monitor.Enter(ref releaseLock);
    ...
    T droppedItem = parent._mode == BoundedChannelFullMode.DropNewest ?
        parent._items.DequeueTail() :
        parent._items.DequeueHead();
    parent._items.EnqueueTail(item);
    Monitor.Exit(parent.SyncObj);
    releaseLock = false;
    _parent._itemDropped?.Invoke(droppedItem);
    return true;
    ...
}
finally
{
    if (releaseLock)
    {
        Monitor.Exit(parent.SyncObj);
    }
}
